# Agent instructions — __CLIENT_FULL__ Salt infrastructure

Working rules for AI agents in this repo (and a short read for humans). `README.md` has the repo
layout, the `drun` / `check_pillar.sh` workflow and onboarding — this file has the rules that are
easy to get wrong.

## This file and `README.md` are template-owned — do not edit them here

Both are regenerated from
[salt-project-template](https://github.com/microdevops-com/salt-project-template)
(`AGENTS.md.example`, `README.md.example`) on **every** template apply. Editing them in this repo
is wasted work twice over: the change is silently overwritten on the next apply, and it never
reaches the other repos that share the template. Do not spend a turn "improving" them here.

| Want to change | Edit |
| --- | --- |
| Project-specific agent rules | `AGENTS.project.md` — appended verbatim to this file, never touched by the template |
| Project-specific human notes | `README.project.md` — appended to `README.md`, never touched by the template |
| The common text in either file | Upstream, in the template repo |

`CLAUDE.md` is a **symlink to this file**, created by the template. Claude Code reads `CLAUDE.md`
natively on every version; `AGENTS.md` is the name Codex and other agents use. One file, one
source of truth. Do not replace the symlink with a real file and do not maintain two copies.

The same "change it upstream" rule applies to every other template-owned path: `Dockerfile`,
`entrypoint.sh`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `scripts/`, `salt/_sdb/`, `etc/salt/master`,
`etc/salt/master.d/`, `etc/salt/minion.d/`, `.check_pillar_for_roster.sh`, `check_pillar.sh`,
`.docker-misc.bash`.
#vault#
#vault### Secrets: the Vault naming schema (mandatory)
#vault#
#vault#Secrets do not go into pillar as plaintext. They live in Vault/OpenBao at
#vault#__VAULT_SALT_SDB_URL__ under `__VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/` and are read at render time through
#vault#the `vault_salt_sdb` sdb driver.
#vault#
#vault#### Referencing a secret from pillar
#vault#
#vault#```
#vault#{% from 'vault_salt_sdb.jinja' import secret %}
#vault#
#vault#postgres:
#vault#  users:
#vault#    webshop:
#vault#      password: "{{ secret('webshop/prod/postgresql/webshop/password') }}"
#vault#```
#vault#
#vault#### The grammar
#vault#
#vault#```
#vault#secret('<area>/<env>/<system>/<instance>/<key>')      exactly five segments, always
#vault#```
#vault#
#vault#which resolves to the Vault path
#vault#`__VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/<area>/<env>/<system>/<instance>`, reading the field `<key>` out of
#vault#that secret.
#vault#
#vault#| Segment | Meaning | Examples |
#vault#| --- | --- | --- |
#vault#| `area` | The product or platform domain that **owns** the credential | `webshop`, `salt`, `gitlab`, `monitoring`, `backup`, `dns`, `mail`, `vpn` |
#vault#| `env` | Closed set: `prod`, `stage`, `dev`, `shared` (`shared` = genuinely environment-independent) | |
#vault#| `system` | The technology or provider that **issued** the credential; `app` when the application itself defines it (signing keys, internal API tokens) | `postgresql`, `mysql`, `redis`, `s3`, `smtp`, `stripe`, `cloudflare`, `registry`, `app` |
#vault#| `instance` | Which one — the role name, account or logical cluster | `webshop`, `hetzner-hel1`, `webhook`, `salt` |
#vault#| `key` | The field inside the secret, `snake_case` | `password`, `token`, `access_key`, `secret_key`, `api_key` |
#vault#
#vault#The depth is fixed on purpose: it is what makes policy globs precise
#vault#(`<mount>/data/<area>/prod/*` scopes one product, `<mount>/data/+/prod/*` scopes all of
#vault#production). Never add or drop a level to make something fit — compound the `instance` with
#vault#`-` instead (`webshop-tmp-prod1`, never `webshop/tmp/prod1`).
#vault#
#vault##### Examples
#vault#
#vault#| What it is | Vault path (under the prefix) | Keys | In pillar |
#vault#| --- | --- | --- | --- |
#vault#| App's database password, production | `webshop/prod/postgresql/webshop` | `password` | `secret('webshop/prod/postgresql/webshop/password')` |
#vault#| Same, staging: only `env` changes | `webshop/stage/postgresql/webshop` | `password` | `secret('webshop/stage/postgresql/webshop/password')` |
#vault#| S3 keys: rotate together, so one secret | `webshop/prod/s3/hetzner-hel1` | `access_key`, `secret_key` | `secret('webshop/prod/s3/hetzner-hel1/secret_key')` |
#vault#| Stripe, one merchant account | `webshop/prod/stripe/acme-doo` | `secret_key`, `webhook_secret` | `secret('webshop/prod/stripe/acme-doo/webhook_secret')` |
#vault#| A secret the app itself defines (session signing) | `webshop/prod/app/session` | `secret` | `secret('webshop/prod/app/session/secret')` |
#vault#| Registry login shared by every app | `gitlab/shared/registry/salt` | `username`, `password` | `secret('gitlab/shared/registry/salt/password')` |
#vault#| DNS API token | `dns/shared/cloudflare/example-com` | `api_token` | `secret('dns/shared/cloudflare/example-com/api_token')` |
#vault#| SMTP relay account | `mail/shared/smtp/sendgrid` | `username`, `password` | `secret('mail/shared/smtp/sendgrid/password')` |
#vault#| Salt master signing key (multi-line PEM) | `salt/prod/master/sign` | `private_key`, `public_key` | `secret('salt/prod/master/sign/private_key')` |
#vault#
#vault##### Common mistakes
#vault#
#vault#| Wrong | Why | Right |
#vault#| --- | --- | --- |
#vault#| `webshop/db_password` | two segments, not five | `webshop/prod/postgresql/webshop/password` |
#vault#| `web1.example.com/postgresql/webshop/password` | hostname; hosts change, the secret doesn't | `webshop/prod/postgresql/webshop/password` |
#vault#| `webshop/production/postgresql/webshop/password` | `env` must be `prod`, `stage`, `dev` or `shared` | `webshop/prod/...` |
#vault#| `webshop/prod/gitlab/registry/password` | the registry login belongs to `gitlab`, not to one of its consumers | `gitlab/shared/registry/salt/password` |
#vault#| `app/webshop/prod/db/password` | mirrors the pillar file path, not the meaning | `webshop/prod/postgresql/webshop/password` |
#vault#| `webshop/prod/s3/access_key` + `webshop/prod/s3/secret_key` as two secrets | fields that rotate together belong in one secret | one secret `webshop/prod/s3/hetzner-hel1`, two keys |
#vault#
#vault#### Vocabulary
#vault#
#vault#The path describes what the secret **is**, never who consumes it. The registry credential that
#vault#every app pulls images with belongs to `gitlab`, not to each app. A database password used by
#vault#both a salt state and the application itself is one secret, stored once.
#vault#
#vault#`area` is an open list, but only deliberately: before inventing one, look at what exists
#vault#(`bao kv list __VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/`) and reuse it. Never coin a synonym for an area that
#vault#is already there (`webshop-app` when `webshop` exists). Platform areas common to every repo are
#vault#`salt`, `gitlab`, `monitoring`, `backup`, `dns`, `mail`, `vpn`; product areas for this repo, if
#vault#listed, are in `AGENTS.project.md`.
#vault#
#vault#### Rules
#vault#
#vault#- Never in a path: hostnames, pillar file names, `.sls`, the consuming state, or any part of a
#vault#  secret value.
#vault#- Lowercase throughout; `-` inside a path segment, `_` inside key names.
#vault#- Group the fields that **rotate together** into one secret: `{access_key, secret_key}` is one
#vault#  path, one version bump, one API call. The driver caches per path, so this is also faster.
#vault#- Record who consumes a secret in `custom_metadata`, not in the path. That is what answers
#vault#  "what breaks if I rotate this".
#vault#- Do not add a new `env` value. The set is closed; widening it is an upstream template change.
#vault#
#vault#### Creating or rotating a secret
#vault#
#vault#Commands below are the OpenBao CLI (`bao`); with a HashiCorp Vault server the same commands are
#vault#`vault ...`.
#vault#
#vault#1. Find the right home before writing anything:
#vault#   ```
#vault#   bao kv list __VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/
#vault#   bao kv list __VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/<area>/<env>/
#vault#   ```
#vault#2. Write the secret, all fields that rotate together in one call:
#vault#   ```
#vault#   bao kv put __VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/<area>/<env>/<system>/<instance> \
#vault#       access_key=... secret_key=...
#vault#   ```
#vault#   **`kv put` replaces the whole secret** — a field you leave out is dropped. To add or change
#vault#   one field of an existing secret, use `bao kv patch` instead.
#vault#3. Record the metadata:
#vault#   ```
#vault#   bao kv metadata put \
#vault#       -custom-metadata=owner=<team-or-person> \
#vault#       -custom-metadata=consumers=<salt-repo,app,...> \
#vault#       -custom-metadata=rotated=<YYYY-MM-DD> \
#vault#       __VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/<area>/<env>/<system>/<instance>
#vault#   ```
#vault#4. Reference it from pillar with `secret(...)` and verify the render: `./check_pillar.sh`.
#vault#
#vault#### Gotchas
#vault#
#vault#- A value cached from an earlier render is reused for `cache_fresh_for` seconds (see
#vault#  `etc/salt/master.d/vault_salt_sdb.conf`), so a just-rotated secret can take a minute to show up
#vault#  locally.
#vault#- KV v2 policy paths carry a `data/` element that the `secret()` path does not:
#vault#  `__VAULT_SALT_SDB_PREFIX__/<area>/...` is read through the policy path
#vault#  `<mount>/data/<rest>/...`. Browsing in the UI additionally needs `list` on `<mount>/metadata/*`.
#vault#- Moving a secret out of pillar does not remove it from git history. Treat a migrated secret as
#vault#  exposed and rotate it unless it was created in Vault in the first place.
